drainage **should NOT be debrided**, as it serves as a natural biological cover (the body''''s ''''natural bandage''''). In all other anatomical locations, eschar generally requires debridement to allow proper staging, assessment, and healing.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 94-old male is admitted to the ICU following a 7-day period of prolonged immobility due to mechanical ventilation. On the second day in the ICU, the nursing staff notices a **localized area of intact skin with a persistent, non-blanchable, deep red, maroon, or purple discoloration** over the right heel. The area feels boggy and painful compared to adjacent tissue. There is no open wound, no blister, and no eschar. Which of the following is the correct classification, and what is the expected clinical evolution of this injury?', '["Stage 1 Pressure Injury; the injury will remain stable and resolve completely with repositioning alone within 24-48 hours", "Arterial insufficiency ulcer; obtain an ankle-brachial index (ABI) and urgent vascular surgery consultation for revascularization", "Stage 2 Pressure Injury; the injury has already progressed to partial-thickness skin loss and requires immediate hydrocolloid application", "Venous stasis ulcer; apply a multi-layer compression bandage and elevate the extremity above the level of the heart", "Deep Tissue Pressure Injury (DTPI); the injury may rapidly evolve to reveal the actual extent of tissue damage, potentially unmasking a Stage 3 or Stage 4 wound within days despite optimal treatment"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This is a **Deep Tissue Pressure Injury (DTPI)**, defined as **intact or non-intact skin with a localized area of persistent, non-blanchable deep red, maroon, or purple discoloration, or epidermal separation revealing a dark wound bed or blood-filled blister**. Pain and temperature change often precede skin color changes. This injury results from **intense and/or prolonged pressure and shear forces at the bone-muscle interface**, causing damage to the underlying soft tissue (muscle, fascia) that then propagates upward toward the skin surface. The critical clinical feature of DTPI is its **unpredictable and often rapid evolution**: despite optimal pressure relief and wound care, the injury may **rapidly