    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-old woman with paraplegia secondary to a spinal cord injury is evaluated for a worsening sacral wound. On examination, the wound reveals **full-thickness skin loss** with visible subcutaneous adipose (fat) tissue in the wound bed. There is a moderate amount of yellow slough but no eschar. Fascia, muscle, tendon, and bone are **not** directly visible or palpable. The wound measures **5 cm x 5 cm x 1 cm** deep. Which of the following is the correct staging, and what is the most critical element of wound bed preparation?', '["Stage 2 Pressure Injury (partial-thickness skin loss); apply a transparent film dressing and observe weekly", "Stage 3 Pressure Injury (full-thickness skin loss with visible fat); wound bed preparation requires debridement of slough to allow granulation tissue formation", "Deep Tissue Pressure Injury; apply a hydrocolloid dressing and reassess in 48 hours for evolution", "Stage 4 Pressure Injury (full-thickness tissue loss with exposed bone); perform an immediate bone biopsy to rule out osteomyelitis", "Unstageable Pressure Injury; the wound cannot be staged until all necrotic tissue and eschar are removed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This wound is a **Stage 3 Pressure Injury**, defined as **full-thickness skin loss** in which adipose (fat) tissue is visible in the ulcer, and granulation tissue or rolled wound edges (epibole) are often present. **Slough** may be present but does not obscure the depth of tissue loss. Fascia, muscle, tendon, ligament, cartilage, and/or bone are **not exposed**. The depth of a Stage 3 pressure injury varies by anatomical location; it can be very shallow in areas without subcutaneous tissue (e.g., the bridge of the nose, ear, occiput) but very deep in areas with significant adiposity. The most critical element of wound bed preparation is **debridement of non-viable tissue (slough)** to create a clean wound bed that supports granulation tissue formation. Debridement methods include autolytic (moisture-retentive dressings), enzymatic (collagenase/Santyl), mechanical, or sharp/surgical debridement depending on the wound characteristics.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-old female with advanced dementia who has been bedbound for 8 months is evaluated for a large sacral wound. On examination, the wound shows **full-thickness tissue loss with directly visible and palpable bone (the sacral periosteum)**. Exposed necrotic muscle and tendon are present at the wound base. There is evidence of undermining and tunneling extending 5 cm laterally. The wound measures **3 cm x 4 cm x 3 cm** deep. Which of the following is the correct staging and what complication must be urgently evaluated?', '["Stage 2 Pressure Injury (partial-thickness loss); apply a hydrocolloid dressing and schedule outpatient follow-up", "Deep Tissue Pressure Injury; observe for 72 hours to determine if the injury will evolve into a higher stage", "Unstageable Pressure Injury; remove all eschar with enzymatic debridement before attempting to stage the wound", "Stage 4 Pressure Injury (full-thickness tissue loss with exposed bone/muscle/tendon); urgently evaluate for osteomyelitis with ESR, CRP, MRI, and consider bone biopsy", "Stage 3 Pressure Injury (full-thickness skin loss with visible fat); apply negative pressure wound therapy (wound VAC) and reassess in 1 week"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This wound is a **Stage 4 Pressure Injury**, the most severe stage, defined as **full-thickness skin and tissue loss with exposed or directly palpable fascia, muscle, tendon, ligament, cartilage, or bone** in the ulcer. Slough and/or eschar may be present, and undermining and tunneling often occur. When **bone is directly visible or palpable** at the wound base (a positive ''''probe-to-bone'''' test), the most critical complication to urgently evaluate is **osteomyelitis** (infection of the underlying bone). Diagnostic evaluation includes: (1) inflammatory markers (**ESR** and **CRP**, both elevated in osteomyelitis), (2) **MRI of the affected area** (the imaging modality of choice, showing bone marrow edema and periosteal enhancement), and (3) **bone biopsy with culture** (the gold standard for definitive diagnosis and guiding antibiotic therapy). Osteomyelitis in pressure injuries typically requires prolonged IV antibiotic courses (6-8 weeks) and surgical debridement of necrotic bone.');
